### What is the Cyclic Sort approach for finding a missing number?

Because the array of length n contains distinct values from 0 to n with exactly one missing, each value v should sit at index v. The Cyclic Sort pass swaps each value to its correct position, and a follow-up scan finds the index where the value does not match.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MissingNumberCyclicSort {
    public int findMissing(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        // Placement pass
        int i = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            if (nums[i] < n && nums[i] != nums[nums[i]]) {
                int temp = nums[nums[i]];
                nums[nums[i]] = nums[i];
                nums[i] = temp;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        // Detection scan
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i) return i;
        }
        return n; // missing is n itself
    }
}
```

### Why skip values equal to n?

The array has length n, so valid indices are 0 through n-1. A value equal to n has no home index. During the placement pass, we simply skip it. After placement, if n is the missing number, every index 0 through n-1 will have its correct value, and the detection scan reaches the end without finding a mismatch.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SkipNDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] slots = {3, 0, 1}; // n=3, range 0..3, missing=2
        // Value 3 == n, skip it during placement
        // After sort: [0, 1, 3] -> index 2 holds 3 != 2 -> missing is 2
        MissingNumberCyclicSort solver = new MissingNumberCyclicSort();
        System.out.println("Missing: " + solver.findMissing(slots)); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
Missing: 2
```

### How does the placement pass work step by step?

The placement pass uses a while loop. At each position, if the value is less than n and not already at its correct index, we swap it to its home. Otherwise, we move to the next position. Each element is swapped at most once, giving O(n) total swaps.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PlacementTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {4, 0, 3, 1}; // n=4, missing=2
        int n = nums.length;
        int i = 0;
        int step = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            if (nums[i] < n && nums[i] != nums[nums[i]]) {
                int target = nums[i];
                int temp = nums[target];
                nums[target] = nums[i];
                nums[i] = temp;
                step++;
                System.out.println("Step " + step + ": swapped, array="
                    + java.util.Arrays.toString(nums));
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        // Final: [0, 1, 3, 3] or similar
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i) {
                System.out.println("Missing: " + i);
                return;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Missing: " + n);
    }
}
```

### How is this different from the sum and XOR methods?

All three methods solve the problem in O(n) time and O(1) space, but Cyclic Sort has the advantage of generalizing to harder variants like finding all missing numbers or detecting duplicates.

| Aspect | Cyclic Sort | Sum Formula | XOR |
|---|---|---|---|
| Overflow risk | None | Yes (use `long`) | None |
| Generalization | All missing, duplicates | Two missing (with sum-of-squares) | Limited |
| Modifies input | Yes | No | No |
| Readability | Moderate | Very simple | Requires XOR knowledge |

```java
// Sum: simple but risks overflow
long expected = (long) n * (n + 1) / 2;

// XOR: no overflow but limited generalization
int xor = 0;
for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) xor ^= j;
for (int val : nums) xor ^= val;

// Cyclic Sort: generalizes to find-all-missing and find-duplicates
while (i < n) { /* swap to home index */ }
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single-element array where the missing number is 0 or 1, and the case where n itself is the missing number.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CyclicSortEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MissingNumberCyclicSort solver = new MissingNumberCyclicSort();

        // Missing is 0
        System.out.println(solver.findMissing(new int[]{1}));       // 0

        // Missing is n
        System.out.println(solver.findMissing(new int[]{0}));       // 1

        // Larger array, missing in middle
        System.out.println(solver.findMissing(new int[]{0,1,3}));   // 2

        // Missing is n (all 0..n-1 present)
        System.out.println(solver.findMissing(new int[]{0,1,2}));   // 3
    }
}
```

```text
0
1
2
3
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not handling the value-equals-n case, causing an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Another mistake is using `nums[i] != i` as the swap guard instead of `nums[i] != nums[nums[i]]`, which fails to detect when the destination already holds the correct value.

```java
// WRONG: no bounds check for value == n
while (i < n) {
    if (nums[i] != i) {  // tries to access nums[n] -> AIOOBE!
        swap(nums, i, nums[i]);
    } else { i++; }
}

// CORRECT: check nums[i] < n before accessing
while (i < n) {
    if (nums[i] < n && nums[i] != nums[nums[i]]) {
        swap(nums, i, nums[i]);
    } else { i++; }
}
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, returned cheque booklets carry leaf numbers in a known range. One leaf is missing from a booklet. The audit clerk places each leaf at its own slot number and reports the mismatched slot, finding the missing leaf in linear time with no extra memory.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BookletAuditService {
    public int findMissingLeaf(int[] returnedLeaves) {
        int n = returnedLeaves.length;
        int i = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            if (returnedLeaves[i] < n
                    && returnedLeaves[i] != returnedLeaves[returnedLeaves[i]]) {
                int target = returnedLeaves[i];
                int temp = returnedLeaves[target];
                returnedLeaves[target] = returnedLeaves[i];
                returnedLeaves[i] = temp;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (returnedLeaves[i] != i) return i;
        }
        return n;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognise that Cyclic Sort is a family of patterns, not just one trick. The same placement loop solves find-one-missing, find-all-missing, find-one-duplicate, and find-all-duplicates. Learning the pattern once covers the whole family. They should also know that the in-place swap modifies the input, which may not be acceptable in all contexts.

```java
// Pattern family:
// 1. Find one missing:  place v at index v, scan for mismatch
// 2. Find all missing:  same placement, collect all mismatches
// 3. Find one duplicate: placement stops when home already occupied
// 4. Find all duplicates: same placement, collect all occupied-home cases
```
